  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Linear models form the backbone of most estimation procedures in quantitative genetics and will be extensively used throughout the rest of this book. They are generally structured such that a vector of observations of one variable (y) is modeled as a linear combination of other variables observed along with y. The remainder of this chapter introduces some of the basic tools and key concepts underlying the use of linear models. Advanced topics are examined in detail in Chapters 26 and 27, and further comments are given in Appendix 3.",
      "source_page": 214,
      "printed_page": 194,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter8:p022:b10",
        "Genetics_chapter8:p022:b11"
      ],
